Write $x/(x-1)=1+1/(x-1)$. The graph is a translated <hyperbola>, with vertical <asymptote> $x=1$ and horizontal <asymptote> $y=1$. It passes through $(0,0)$, lies below $y=1$ for $x<1$ and above it for $x>1$, and has <derivative> $-1/(x-1)^2<0$ on each component of its domain. The left panel below shows both branches.

\Image[paper-1-elementary-graphs.png]
{title=The rational hyperbola and the convex graph of the logarithmic product}

= ii
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The domain is $x>0$, and $x\log x\to0$ from below as $x\downarrow0$. Its <derivative> is $1+\log x$, so it decreases up to $x=e^{-1}$ and then increases, with its unique global <minimum> at $(e^{-1},-e^{-1})$. It crosses the horizontal axis at $(1,0)$ and tends to $+\infty$ as $x\to\infty$. Its <second derivative> is $1/x>0$, so it is strictly <convex>. The right panel of the preceding figure shows these features; zero is an excluded endpoint with a finite limiting value, rather than a vertical <asymptote>.

= 8
{parent=Paper 1}
