Multiplying by $z^2-1$ gives $z^6-1=0$. The sixth <roots of unity> $1,-1$ do not solve the original equation, since substituting either gives three. The other four do, because the extra factor is nonzero. Hence
$$
\boxed{z=e^{ik\pi/3},\qquad k=1,2,4,5.}
$$
Their <real parts> are $\pm1/2$ and their <imaginary parts> are $\pm\sqrt3/2$, giving the four corners in the left <Argand diagram> below.

\Image[paper-1-complex-roots.png]
{title=Four roots on the unit circle and the two horizontal rows of complex sine solutions}

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Writing $z=x+iy$ and comparing <real parts> and <imaginary parts> gives $\sin x\cosh y=\cosh2$ and $\cos x\sinh y=0$. A real solution $y=0$ is impossible since $\cosh2>1$. Thus $\cos x=0$, and positivity forces $\sin x=1$. Consequently $x=\pi/2+2k\pi$ and $\cosh y=\cosh2$, giving
$$
\boxed{z=\frac\pi2+2k\pi\pm2i,\qquad k\in\mathbb Z.}
$$
The right <Argand diagram> in the preceding figure shows representative points on the lines $\operatorname{Im}z=\pm2$; each row continues with spacing $2\pi$.

