= Solution
{parent=i}

The <separable differential equation> gives $d(1-y)/(1-y)=-3\,dx/x$. Thus $1-y=Cx^{-3}$ on $x>0$, and the initial value gives $C=1$. Hence
$$
\boxed{y=1-x^{-3},\qquad x>0.}
$$
The initial-value branch cannot cross its singularity at zero.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Multiplication by $e^y$ gives $d(e^y)/dx=x/\sqrt{1+x^2}$. Integrating and imposing $y(0)=0$ yields $e^y=\sqrt{1+x^2}$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{y=\tfrac12\log(1+x^2),\qquad x\in\mathbb R.}
$$

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

On an interval avoiding zero put $v=y/x$. A real solution requires $|v|\leq1$, and the principal <square root> is $\sqrt{x^2-y^2}=|x|\sqrt{1-v^2}$. The <homogeneous first-order differential equation> reduces to
$$
v'=\frac{\sqrt{1-v^2}}{|x|}.
$$
Where $|v|<1$, <separation of variables> gives $\arcsin v=\operatorname{sgn}(x)\log|x|+C$. Thus the nonconstant pieces are
$$
\boxed{y=x\sin\bigl(\operatorname{sgn}(x)\log|x|+C\bigr),}
$$
restricted to intervals where the cosine of the phase is nonnegative. This sign condition is essential when substituting back into the original <square root>.

There are also the solutions $y=x$ and $y=-x$. They can join the nonconstant pieces with a continuous first <derivative>, because the transformed rate vanishes but is not <Lipschitz continuous> at $v=\pm1$. For example, on the positive axis every $A>0$ gives
$$
y(x)=\begin{cases}-x,&0<x\leq A,\\x\sin\bigl(\log(x/A)-\pi/2\bigr),&A<x<Ae^\pi,\\x,&x\geq Ae^\pi.\end{cases}
$$
The <sticking solutions of a bounded square-root differential equation> describe the same joining mechanism on either half-axis after using the increasing variable $t=\operatorname{sgn}(x)\log|x|$.

= b
{parent=16}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Near the initial point, division by $\cos x$ gives $y'+(1+\tan x)y=2\sec x+2\sin x$. An <integrating factor> is $e^x\sec x$, and
$$
\frac{d}{dx}(e^x\sec x\,y)=2e^x\sec^2x+2e^x\tan x=\frac{d}{dx}(2e^x\tan x).
$$
Thus $y=2\sin x+Ce^{-x}\cos x$, and $y(0)=2$ gives
$$
\boxed{y=2\sin x+2e^{-x}\cos x.}
$$
Although division by $\cos x$ was local, direct substitution verifies this smooth solution in the original equation for every real $x$, including the zeros of $\cos x$.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Set $u=1/y$, so $u'=-y'/y^2$. The equation becomes $x(x^2-1)u'+u=-x^3$. For $x>1$ its <integrating factor> is $\sqrt{x^2-1}/x$, giving
$$
\frac{d}{dx}\left(\frac{\sqrt{x^2-1}}x u\right)=-\frac{x}{\sqrt{x^2-1}},\qquad
u=-x+\frac{Cx}{\sqrt{x^2-1}}.
$$
The initial value is $u(2)=-2$, forcing $C=0$. Hence
$$
\boxed{y=-\frac1x.}
$$
Substitution into the original equation verifies this answer throughout $x>0$, including $x=1$ where the divided linear equation was singular. Its maximal connected interval through the initial point is $(0,\infty)$.

= 17
{parent=Paper 1}
