= Solution
{parent=i}

The zero set consists of the three lines $x=0$, $y=0$ and $y=x-1$. Their pairwise intersections are
$$
\boxed{(0,0),\quad(1,0),\quad(0,-1).}
$$
The bounded <triangle> has $0\leq x\leq1$ and $x-1\leq y\leq0$. Its edges and interior <contours> are shown below.

\Image[paper-1-triangular-contours.png]
{title=Negative contours inside the zero triangle, with outward boundary gradients and the interior minimum}

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <gradient> is $\nabla f=(y(1-2x+y),x(1-x+2y))$. At the three specified points it is respectively
$$
\boxed{(0,1/4),\qquad(1/4,-1/4),\qquad(-1/4,0).}
$$
Each <vector> is perpendicular to its straight boundary edge and points out of the <triangle>, where $f$ increases from its negative interior values. The arrows in the preceding figure mark these directions.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Inside the <triangle>, both $x$ and $y$ are nonzero. Thus $\nabla f=0$ reduces to $1-2x+y=0$ and $1-x+2y=0$, whose unique solution is $(1/3,-1/3)$. At that point
$$
H=\begin{pmatrix}2/3&-1/3\\-1/3&2/3\end{pmatrix},\qquad f=-\frac1{27}.
$$
The <Hessian matrix> has positive <eigenvalues> $1$ and $1/3$, so this is a strict local <minimum>. It is also the minimum on the closed <triangle>: $f=0$ on the boundary, $f<0$ inside, and there is no other interior <stationary point>. Therefore
$$
\boxed{(x,y)=(1/3,-1/3),\qquad f_{\min}=-1/27.}
$$

= iv
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

The interior values satisfy $-1/27<f<0$ away from the <minimum>. The <contours> are closed curves surrounding $(1/3,-1/3)$, approximately <ellipses> near that point, and approach the three edges as the level tends to zero. To see their shape globally, put $u=x$, $v=-y$, $w=1-x+y$. These are positive inside and satisfy $u+v+w=1$, while $f=-uvw$. Since $\log(uvw)=\log u+\log v+\log w$ is strictly <concave>, its nonempty superlevel sets are convex and surround the unique maximum of $uvw$. Thus the negative contours nest without additional components, as in the figure.

= b
{parent=17}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <gradient> is $(-y^2-2x-3x^2,2y(1-x))$. Its second component vanishes only if $y=0$ or $x=1$. The latter gives the impossible first-component equation $-y^2-5=0$. With $y=0$, the first component gives $x=0$ or $x=-2/3$. The <Hessian matrix> is
$$
H_g=\begin{pmatrix}-2-6x&-2y\\-2y&2(1-x)\end{pmatrix}.
$$
At $(0,0)$ it has <eigenvalues> $-2,2$, giving a **saddle point**. At $(-2/3,0)$ its <eigenvalues> are $2,10/3$, giving a **strict local minimum**, of value $-4/27$. These are all real <stationary points>. The minimum is local: for example $g(x,0)\to-\infty$ as $x\to+\infty$.

