Expanding the <determinant> along the first row gives
$$
\boxed{\det S=(3-4)+(0-3)=-4.}
$$

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The matrix of <cofactors> is $\begin{pmatrix}-1&2&-3\\2&0&-2\\-3&-2&3\end{pmatrix}$. Transposing and dividing by the nonzero <determinant> gives the <inverse matrix>
$$
\boxed{S^{-1}=\begin{pmatrix}1/4&-1/2&3/4\\-1/2&0&1/2\\3/4&1/2&-3/4\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
Multiplication by $S$ verifies the <identity matrix>.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Take the <diagonal matrix> $T=\operatorname{diag}(1,0,0)$. Multiplying on the left retains the first row of $S$, while multiplying on the right retains its first column. Thus
$$
TS=\begin{pmatrix}1&0&1\\0&0&0\\0&0&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad
ST=\begin{pmatrix}1&0&0\\0&0&0\\1&0&0\end{pmatrix}.
$$
Their $(1,3)$ entries differ, so this is an explicit example of noncommuting <matrices>.

