{parent=i}

The <Leibniz formula for determinants> gives $\det(A^T)=\sum_{\sigma\in S_3}\operatorname{sgn}(\sigma)\prod_i a_{\sigma(i),i}$. Relabel the product by $j=\sigma(i)$ and then the sum by $\tau=\sigma^{-1}$. The inverse <permutation> has the same sign, so
$$
\det(A^T)=\sum_{\tau\in S_3}\operatorname{sgn}(\tau)\prod_j a_{j,\tau(j)}=\det A.
$$
The proof applies to any finite square <matrix>.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Expanding the <trace> of the product and exchanging the two finite summation indices gives
$$
\operatorname{Tr}(AB)=\sum_{i,j}a_{ij}b_{ji}=\sum_{j,i}b_{ji}a_{ij}=\operatorname{Tr}(BA).
$$
Scalar entries commute even though the <matrices> need not.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Apply the preceding <trace> identity first to the pair $AB,C$ and then to $CA,B$:
$$
\operatorname{Tr}(ABC)=\operatorname{Tr}(CAB)=\operatorname{Tr}(BCA).
$$
This is invariance under cyclic permutation. It does not assert invariance under arbitrary rearrangement of the <matrices>.

