= Solution
{parent=i}

For every $n\geq1$, $|x^n\sin(1/x)|\leq|x|^n\to0$, so the <squeeze theorem> gives limit zero. For $n=0$, sequences $x_k=(\pi/2+2k\pi)^{-1}$ and $\widetilde x_k=(3\pi/2+2k\pi)^{-1}$ give function values $1$ and $-1$, so no limit exists. Hence
$$
\boxed{n=1,2,3,4.}
$$
The value assigned at zero when $n=4$ does not affect this punctured <limit>.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For $n=1,2,3$ the limit zero equals the assigned value at zero. For $n=4$ the limit is zero but the assigned value is one, giving a <removable discontinuity>. For $n=0$ there is no limit. Thus <continuity> at zero holds precisely for
$$
\boxed{n=1,2,3.}
$$

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

For $n=1,2,3$, the difference quotient at zero is $x^{n-1}\sin(1/x)$. It oscillates without a limit for $n=1$, while the <squeeze theorem> gives limit zero for $n=2,3$. The other two cases fail <continuity> and therefore cannot be differentiable. Hence
$$
\boxed{n=2,3,\qquad q_x(0,n)=0.}
$$

