For a series with eventually nonzero terms $a_n$, suppose $r=\lim_{n\to\infty}|a_{n+1}/a_n|$ exists. The <ratio test> says that $r<1$ implies <absolute convergence>, while $r>1$ implies divergence because the terms fail to tend to zero. At $r=1$ the test is **inconclusive**.

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Here $|a_{n+1}/a_n|=n/(n+1)\to1$. Therefore the <ratio test> is **inconclusive**. The series does in fact converge by the <alternating series test>, and fails to converge absolutely because the <harmonic series> diverges; these facts use other tests.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For the positive summand $a_n=(n!)^3e^{3n}/(3n)!$, the <ratio test> gives
$$
\frac{a_{n+1}}{a_n}=\frac{e^3(n+1)^3}{(3n+1)(3n+2)(3n+3)}\longrightarrow\frac{e^3}{27}<1.
$$
Thus the series **converges absolutely**.

